{
 "_doc": [
  "How much does tighter framing fix the gutter split? Measured 2026-08-19 on the four",
  "testset/zoomset_* anchors, which were shot with 40-55% of the frame full of room.",
  "",
  "METHOD: each anchor's book box and true spine x were HAND-READ off a 5% grid (the same",
  "convention testset/skewset_manifest.json uses -- no automatic page-crop route is trusted",
  "on this batch). Each anchor was then cropped to the book box grown by a margin of M x the",
  "book's own size on every side, and pipeline.stage02_split.detect_gutter was re-run on the",
  "crop with the SHIPPED config.yaml params. The detected gutter was mapped back to full-frame",
  "coordinates and compared with the hand-read spine. 'fill' is the book's width as a fraction",
  "of the crop's width -- 1.00 means the book exactly fills the frame edge to edge.",
  "A result counts as correct if it lands within 4% of the book's width of the true spine.",
  "",
  "RESULT: all four spreads split correctly once fill >= 0.83. Three of four are already",
  "correct at fill >= 0.57. As shot (fill 0.47-0.64) only one of four was correct.",
  "",
  "CAVEAT: cropping in software is not the same as standing closer -- it changes framing only,",
  "not perspective or resolution. That is the right proxy for THIS question (the gutter finder",
  "sees framing) and the wrong one for anything about sharpness or dewarp.",
  "",
  "SIDE RESULT: de_01 resolves via the 'pinch' cue at every crop, including the tightest.",
  "The worry that cropping the room away would remove the dark background that cue depends on",
  "did not materialise on this batch. Whether a PALE background (a light sofa rather than a",
  "dark floor) hurts it is NOT tested here."
 ],
